   "line": [
    "Bf4"
   ],
   "spoken": "Which system to use against the London depends entirely on what you already play against the two main queenside openings — a Queen's Gambit player answers with the central pawn, a King's Indian or Grünfeld player fianchettoes, a Benoni player strikes with the c-pawn. Reach for the setup you already know, and the least flexible choice is the passive one.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 4,
   "t": 60.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/ppp1pppp/5n2/3p4/3P1B2/8/PPP1PPPP/RN1QKBNR w KQkq - 0 3",
   "line": [
    "d5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 5,
   "t": 86.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/ppp1pppp/5n2/3p4/3P1B2/4P3/PPP2PPP/RN1QKBNR b KQkq - 0 3",
   "line": [
    "e3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 6,
   "t": 101,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/pp2pppp/5n2/2pp4/3P1B2/4P3/PPP2PPP/RN1QKBNR w KQkq - 0 4",
   "line": [
    "c5"
   ],
   "spoken": "The move that really challenges the London is c5 — it lets the opponent capture, which is ambitious and mainstream, and immediately contests the centre, essentially playing it like a Queen's Gambit a move down. Confrontation, not accommodation.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 106.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/pp2pppp/5n2/2pp4/3P1B2/2P1P3/PP3PPP/RN1QKBNR b KQkq - 0 4",
   "line": [
    "c3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 124,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp2pppp/2n2n2/2pp4/3P1B2/2P1P3/PP3PPP/RN1QKBNR w KQkq - 1 5",
   "line": [
    "Nc6"
   ],
   "spoken": "Keep the tension and develop the knight to c6 — now grabbing the pawn is far less appealing, because a central push with tempo regains it at once. That resource is part of the reason the knight went there.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 153,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp2pppp/2n2n2/2pp4/3P1B2/2P1P3/PP1N1PPP/R2QKBNR b KQkq - 2 5",
   "line": [
    "Nd2"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 10,
   "t": 177.5,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/pp2pppp/1qn2n2/2pp4/3P1B2/2P1P3/PP1N1PPP/R2QKBNR w KQkq - 3 6",
   "line": [
    "Qb6"
   ],
   "spoken": "The queen swings to b6, pressing the pawn and setting up the tension between the queens. This is the move that leads to a structure many opponents in this range struggle to handle.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 11,
   "t": 197,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/pp2pppp/1qn2n2/2pp4/3P1B2/1QP1P3/PP1N1PPP/R3KBNR b KQkq - 4 6",
   "line": [
    "Qb3"
   ],
   "spoken": "Should you trade the queens? No — that would let the opponent recapture with a pawn, and the resulting semi-open file matters enormously; you want to be the side controlling it. So instead encourage the trade on your own terms with the pawn to c4.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 214,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/pp2pppp/1Qn2n2/3p4/2pP1B2/2P1P3/PP1N1PPP/R3KBNR b KQkq - 0 7",
   "line": [
    "c4",
    "Qxb6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 215,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/1p2pppp/1pn2n2/3p4/2pP1B2/2P1P3/PP1N1PPP/R3KBNR w KQkq - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "axb6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 15,
   "t": 219.5,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/1p2pppp/1pn2n2/3p4/2pP1B2/P1P1P3/1P1N1PPP/R3KBNR b KQkq - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "a3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 257.5,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/1p2pppp/2n2n2/1p1p4/2pP1B2/P1P1P3/1P1N1PPP/R3KBNR w KQkq - 0 9",
   "line": [
    "b5"
   ],
   "spoken": "The typical plan hinges on a detail — the enemy rook in the corner is undefended, so there's a standoff between the rooks. Exploit it to steamroll the queenside with b5, which also supports the advanced c-pawn and threatens to break through, the follow-up knight capture being untouchable because taking it would drop the hanging rook.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
  {
   "ply": 17,
   "t": 260.5,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/1p2pppp/2n2n2/1p1p4/2pPPB2/P1P5/1P1N1PPP/R3KBNR b KQkq - 0 9",
   "line": [
    "e4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 265,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/1p2pppp/2n2n2/1p1p4/2pP1B2/P1P1P3/1P1N1PPP/R3KBNR w KQkq - 0 9",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": "The concrete threat is to advance and capture on the b-file, then recapture with the knight, smashing through the first layer of the defence — and the knight can't be taken, since the rook behind falls. A pawn break backed by a tactical point.",
   "kind": "tactic",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 17,
   "t": 280,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/1p2pppp/2n2n2/1p1p4/2pPPB2/P1P5/1P1N1PPP/R3KBNR b KQkq - 0 9",
   "line": [
    "e4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 18,
   "t": 294,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/1p2pppp/2n2n2/1p6/2pPpB2/P1P5/1P1N1PPP/R3KBNR w KQkq - 0 10",
   "line": [
    "dxe4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 19,
   "t": 297.5,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/1p2pppp/2n2n2/1p4B1/2pPp3/P1P5/1P1N1PPP/R3KBNR b KQkq - 1 10",
   "line": [
    "Bg5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 331,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/1p2pppp/2n2n2/1p1p4/2pP1B2/P1P1P3/1P1N1PPP/R3KBNR w KQkq - 0 9",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 20,
   "t": 336.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1p2pppp/2n2n2/1p3bB1/2pPp3/P1P5/1P1N1PPP/R3KBNR w KQkq - 0 11",
   "line": [
    "Bg5",
    "Bf5",
    "e4",
    "dxe4"
   ],
   "spoken": "The situation has shifted — with the centre changed, the queenside break makes less sense because the advanced pawn is now loose. So refocus on holding the extra central pawn, developing the bishop to f5 to defend it. Re-evaluate what's hanging every time the structure changes.",
   "kind": "plan",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 21,
   "t": 341.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1p2pppp/2n2n2/1p3bB1/2pPp3/P1P5/1P1NNPPP/R3KB1R b KQkq - 1 11",
   "line": [
    "Ne2"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 22,
   "t": 353.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1p2pppp/2n2nb1/1p4B1/2pPp3/P1P5/1P1NNPPP/R3KB1R w KQkq - 2 12",
   "line": [
    "Bg6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 23,
   "t": 359.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1p2pppp/2n2nb1/1p4B1/2pPp3/P1P3N1/1P1N1PPP/R3KB1R b KQkq - 3 12",
   "line": [
    "Ng3"
   ],
   "spoken": "Drop the bishop back to g6 as a prophylactic probe — it makes the opponent show their hand before you commit. And take stock: a lead in development, a space edge, better-placed pieces, and an extra pawn the opponent must spend several moves just to regain.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 24,
   "t": 404.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1p3ppp/2n2nb1/1p2p1B1/2pPp3/P1P3N1/1P1N1PPP/R3KB1R w KQkq - 0 13",
   "line": [
    "e5"
   ],
   "spoken": "The rule with a space advantage and better development is to open the centre — and since the queenside break no longer works, the way to do it is the pawn to e5. Once the opponent gives up a bishop, the resulting bishop pair in an open position becomes hugely valuable.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 25,
   "t": 424.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1p3ppp/2n2Bb1/1p2p3/2pPp3/P1P3N1/1P1N1PPP/R3KB1R b KQkq - 0 13",
   "line": [
    "Bxf6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 26,
   "t": 425.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1p3p1p/2n2pb1/1p2p3/2pPp3/P1P3N1/1P1N1PPP/R3KB1R w KQkq - 0 14",
   "line": [
    "gxf6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 27,
   "t": 434,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1p3p1p/2n2pb1/1p2P3/2p1p3/P1P3N1/1P1N1PPP/R3KB1R b KQkq - 0 14",
   "line": [
    "dxe5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 28,
   "t": 464,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1p3p1p/2n3b1/1p2Pp2/2p1p3/P1P3N1/1P1N1PPP/R3KB1R w KQkq - 0 15",
   "line": [
    "f5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Look closely at the centre for a way to resolve the tension in your favour — the advanced enemy pawn is a sitting duck. A pawn to f5 props your own central pawn, forms a chain, and leaves that loose pawn simply doomed. The best of all worlds.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 29,
   "t": 472.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1p3p1p/2n3b1/1p2Pp2/2p1p2P/P1P3N1/1P1N1PP1/R3KB1R b KQkq - 0 15",
   "line": [
    "h4"
   ],
   "spoken": "Stay alert — the opponent wants to advance the rook-pawn to trap the bishop, so pre-empt it by pushing your own rook-pawn to the same square. Meet the threat before it arrives.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 30,
   "t": 477,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1p3p2/2n3b1/1p2Pp1p/2p1p2P/P1P3N1/1P1N1PP1/R3KB1R w KQkq - 0 16",
   "line": [
    "h5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 31,
   "t": 484,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1p3p2/2n3b1/1p2Pp1p/2p1p2P/P1P2PN1/1P1N2P1/R3KB1R b KQkq - 0 16",
   "line": [
    "f3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 32,
   "t": 509,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1p3p2/2n3b1/1p2Pp1p/2p4P/P1P1pPN1/1P1N2P1/R3KB1R w KQkq - 0 17",
   "line": [
    "e3"
   ],
   "spoken": "Don't snatch the pawn on autopilot, and above all don't take toward the centre, which would let the enemy knight back into the game. The natural move keeps the knight shut out, and the follow-up builds a pawn chain that locks in the advantage.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
  {
   "ply": 33,
   "t": 518.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1p3p2/2n3b1/1p2Pp1p/2p4P/P1P1pPN1/1P4P1/RN2KB1R b KQkq - 1 17",
   "line": [
    "Nb1"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 34,
   "t": 519.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1p3p2/2n3b1/1p2P2p/2p2p1P/P1P1pPN1/1P4P1/RN2KB1R w KQkq - 0 18",
   "line": [
    "f4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 35,
   "t": 528,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1p3p2/2n3b1/1p2P2p/2p2p1P/P1P1pP2/1P2N1P1/RN2KB1R b KQkq - 1 18",
   "line": [
    "Ne2"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 36,
   "t": 549,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1p3p2/2n3bb/1p2P2p/2p2p1P/P1P1pP2/1P2N1P1/RN2KB1R w KQkq - 2 19",
   "line": [
    "Bh6"
   ],
   "spoken": "The base of the pawn chain is the key square to protect — lose that pawn and the whole chain starts to unravel. The simplest way is to develop the bishop out to h6, defending it from a distance.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 37,
   "t": 552,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1p3p2/2n3bb/1p2P2p/2pN1p1P/P1P1pP2/1P4P1/RN2KB1R b KQkq - 3 19",
   "line": [
    "Nd4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 38,
   "t": 589,
   "fen": "r3k2r/1p3p2/6bb/1p2n2p/2pN1p1P/P1P1pP2/1P4P1/RN2KB1R w KQkq - 0 20",
   "line": [
    "Nxe5"
   ],
   "spoken": "The cleanest finish is the less materialistic one — capturing on e5 and swinging the knight around toward d3, where it lands with devastating effect. Don't cling to a pawn count when a piece invasion decides the game.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
  {
